WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.365
The main rule of this course is validate
before trust.

2
00:00:03.445 --> 00:00:07.838
Input is not trusted just because it was
received, decoded, copied, or converted.

3
00:00:07.918 --> 00:00:11.865
It becomes usable only after it has been
checked against explicit source,

4
00:00:11.945 --> 00:00:14.110
type, range, length, format, encoding,

5
00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:16.991
and state rules for the operation it will
influence.

6
00:00:17.071 --> 00:00:18.854
Use practical review questions.

7
00:00:18.934 --> 00:00:20.589
Where did this input come from?

8
00:00:20.669 --> 00:00:22.452
What trust boundary did it cross?

9
00:00:22.532 --> 00:00:25.842
What type, range, length, format, and
encoding are expected?

10
00:00:25.922 --> 00:00:27.896
Has the input been parsed before use?

11
00:00:27.976 --> 00:00:31.987
Could invalid input influence memory
access, resource use, control flow,

12
00:00:32.067 --> 00:00:33.022
or state changes?

13
00:00:33.102 --> 00:00:36.094
When validation fails, the program should
fail closed,

14
00:00:36.174 --> 00:00:40.249
avoid partial state updates, clean up
temporary resources, and log safely.

15
00:00:40.329 --> 00:00:44.212
Deeper code should receive a validated
representation or a clear error,

16
00:00:44.292 --> 00:00:47.284
not ambiguous data that every layer has to
reinterpret.

17
00:00:47.364 --> 00:00:50.611
Finally, keep validation alive through
testing and review.

18
00:00:50.691 --> 00:00:54.448
Boundary tests, malformed input tests,
regression tests, warnings,

19
00:00:54.528 --> 00:00:59.175
static analysis, sanitizers, and
high-level input-generation workflows can reveal

20
00:00:59.255 --> 00:01:00.401
missed assumptions.

21
00:01:00.481 --> 00:01:04.874
They work best when the validation
contract is explicit enough for both humans and

22
00:01:04.954 --> 00:01:06.100
tools to understand.
